  "body": "Polyfills are not going to go away, they will be part of the web forever, since not all browsers implement the same features at the same pace.\r\nHowever, polyfills can unnecessarily slow down loading, because they are usually loaded synchronously, before any code is run.\r\nIn order not to slow down browsers that do not need them, using this proposal, they can be loaded conditionally, but still synchronously (and without using the horrible `document.write` and the like).\r\n\r\nI propose something along these lines -\r\n```html\r\n<script src=\"object-entries-polyfill.js\" conditional>\r\nreturn Object.entries;\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis way, scripts can be loaded only if the browser does not support a feature, according to feature detection.\r\nWhen a script has a `conditional` attribute, the content of the tag will be evaluated (as a body of a function, like inline event listeners) before fetching the script from `src` and if it returns a `false`-like value or throws an exception, the script must be fetched, otherwise, it should not be fetched and it must not be evaluated.\r\nWhy \"should not be fetched\" and not \"must not be fetched\"? Pre-parse/load scanners can choose to fetch the script anyway, but not evaluate (or block on it), as an optimization.",
